{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 993 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "eaf3c9c1a9e374b747b71a59baae6098ce366c6c19d15dd32739cf0759464e0d" :edb/db-before-t 992 :edb/db-after-t 993 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534305 50 #inst "2026-10-11T18:49:36.513Z" 13194139534305 17592186045417 true] [17592186119627 1071 "The matrices $A$ and $B$ are given below. If $A$ is row equivalent to $B$, then find a basis of $\\textrm{Null}(A).$\n\n![](images/51/517c8b69f022d892daa4f8f48a74f6a93b12143e71f5077a046133b1b396ee9f.svg)" 13194139534305 17592186045417 true] [17592186119627 1071 "The matrices $A$ and $B$ are given below. If $A$ is row equivalent to $B$, then find a basis of $\\textrm{Null}(A).$\n\n![](images/59/5971723fe2da8b35c0c965b25bf084080b2602b52a1099b6c97f637ca62fb2c2.png)" 13194139534305 17592186045417 false] [17592186119627 1074 "To find a basis of $\\textrm{Null}(A),$ we need to solve the equation $A\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{0}$ and express the solution as a linear combination of linearly independent vectors. Those vectors form a basis of $\\textrm{Null}(A).$\n\nSince $A\\sim B,$ the equation $A \\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{0}$ is equivalent to the equation $B\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0},$ which corresponds to the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}-\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{3}+\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{4}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is $x_1=0$ and $x_2=\\dfrac{1}{3}x_3 - \\dfrac{2}{3} x_4$ where $x_3$ and $x_4$ are free variables. Therefore, if $\\mathbf{x}\\in\\textrm{Null}(A),$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}{x}_{3}-\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{4} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{4}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3},{x}_{4}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis implies that\n\n![](images/50/50bf947d3beddfb5be8eee27c0d77bf57f3e93204a8554ffffa1e4cc41f35956.svg)\n\nis a basis of $\\textrm{Null}(A)$ since it is a linearly independent set and its vectors generate $\\textrm{Null}(A).$" 13194139534305 17592186045417 true] [17592186119627 1074 "To find a basis of $\\textrm{Null}(A),$ we need to solve the equation $A\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{0}$ and express the solution as a linear combination of linearly independent vectors. Those vectors form a basis of $\\textrm{Null}(A).$\n\nSince $A\\sim B,$ the equation $A \\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{0}$ is equivalent to the equation $B\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0},$ which corresponds to the following system:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}-\\frac{1}{3}{x}_{3}+\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{4}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the general solution is $x_1=0$ and $x_2=\\dfrac{1}{3}x_3 - \\dfrac{2}{3} x_4$ where $x_3$ and $x_4$ are free variables. Therefore, if $\\mathbf{x}\\in\\textrm{Null}(A),$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}{x}_{3}-\\frac{2}{3}{x}_{4} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{4}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -\\frac{2}{3} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{3},{x}_{4}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis implies that\n\n![](images/7c/7c7c6711d4453e0e8365dbec263cf23e0c8e4bf05ff091ecc3bf9593797ff538.png)\n\nis a basis of $\\textrm{Null}(A)$ since it is a linearly independent set and its vectors generate $\\textrm{Null}(A).$" 13194139534305 17592186045417 false] [17592186119628 1071 "Given that  ![](images/41/412e5084f88d0eb270b0e8a1f5613f85e1cec2c9f89879ccda30ff4c0794fb3d.png) find a basis for $\\textrm{Null}(A).$" 13194139534305 17592186045417 false] [17592186119628 1071 "Given that  ![](images/b4/b489811024ece2341b91e0b9819714a988cf31a87598601a8a1257c02129698c.svg) find a basis for $\\textrm{Null}(A).$" 13194139534305 17592186045417 true] [17592186119628 1074 "Any vector in $\\textrm{Null}(A)$ has the form\n\n![](images/7b/7bba2b6227c4fe42901e654d6025c54d7937c13743dfc74586fc72c767c31c06.png)\n\nExpanding, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{3}-{x}_{4} \\\\ 4{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{3} \\\\ 4{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{4} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{4}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a basis for $\\textrm{Null}(A)$ is\n\n![](images/60/60eca2d19c167355d263c471cb876e181db49ab5ac65b27561e6fb95bcc21ec0.png)\n\nsince it is a linearly independent set and its vectors generate $\\textrm{Null}(A).$" 13194139534305 17592186045417 false] [17592186119628 1074 "Any vector in $\\textrm{Null}(A)$ has the form\n\n![](images/94/94af7adea734ab0f38b5f8521fcf575cbc263cc1700a3144f682706711604859.svg)\n\nExpanding, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{3}-{x}_{4} \\\\ 4{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{3} \\\\ 4{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-{x}_{4} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{4}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, a basis for $\\textrm{Null}(A)$ is\n\n![](images/4a/4a8f749660a2c8b71cd9fe61c97180fd143a3c06c754e75a1dc51c14622f03a2.svg)\n\nsince it is a linearly independent set and its vectors generate $\\textrm{Null}(A).$" 13194139534305 17592186045417 true] [17592186119629 1071 "Find a basis of the null space of the matrix ![](images/35/35651f90fba8141cafc7d513670e9903a4977a2c4bd3a8317ee7be90f4e7c493.svg)" 13194139534305 17592186045417 true] [17592186119629 1071 "Find a basis of the null space of the matrix ![](images/e8/e85d4892dc0bad0c6019b0434eff3d140fcf654171f6fda480b109b324e7b443.png)" 13194139534305 17592186045417 false] [17592186119629 1074 "To find a basis of $\\textrm{Null}(A),$ we need to solve the equation $A\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{0}$ and express the solution as a linear combination of linearly independent vectors. Those vectors form a basis of $\\textrm{Null}(A).$\n\nThe given matrix is already in reduced row echelon form. So, the equation $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ corresponds to the following system:\n![](images/36/367b1779ba31df69791a24ab485f1715cedb46f57e383053fd17a189c465d09a.png)\n\nHere, $x_1$ and $x_3$ are basic variables corresponding to the pivot columns ($1$st and $3$rd) and $x_2,x_4$ are free variables corresponding to the non-pivot columns. Therefore, if $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\textrm{Null}(A),$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-3{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ 4{x}_{4} \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4{x}_{4} \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{4}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $x_2,x_4\\in\\mathbb{R}.$ This implies that\n\n![](images/9b/9b2704bbd684106b7c1c7dbe52eb957e65e6634d9adb6ba7fb415b0097af7e64.png)\n\nis a basis of $\\textrm{Null}(A)$ since it is a linearly independent set and its vectors generate $\\textrm{Null}(A).$" 13194139534305 17592186045417 false] [17592186119629 1074 "To find a basis of $\\textrm{Null}(A),$ we need to solve the equation $A\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{0}$ and express the solution as a linear combination of linearly independent vectors. Those vectors form a basis of $\\textrm{Null}(A).$\n\nThe given matrix is already in reduced row echelon form. So, the equation $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ corresponds to the following system:\n![](images/af/afdd7aba38057c3f4b226c63d103c83d3a76171ae8ae2a900db4c875a9c4e023.svg)\n\nHere, $x_1$ and $x_3$ are basic variables corresponding to the pivot columns ($1$st and $3$rd) and $x_2,x_4$ are free variables corresponding to the non-pivot columns. Therefore, if $\\mathbf{x} \\in \\textrm{Null}(A),$ then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-3{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ 4{x}_{4} \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4{x}_{4} \\\\ {x}_{4}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{4}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $x_2,x_4\\in\\mathbb{R}.$ This implies that\n\n![](images/4d/4d430991c440bc6e0afad527930566f4c839476f8f1c4f1ee91f2f726d0ece27.svg)\n\nis a basis of $\\textrm{Null}(A)$ since it is a linearly independent set and its vectors generate $\\textrm{Null}(A).$" 13194139534305 17592186045417 true] [17592186119630 1071 "Given the matrix ![](images/13/13a1fbdcd6620ab5ccc41283da5ded8996053f9a59fd8c820ea786837ae05222.png) find a basis of $\\textrm{Null}(A).$" 13194139534305 17592186045417 false] [17592186119630 1071 "Given the matrix ![](images/ec/ec919391ecd75553fc12f04d1cb6832c7019677ff0954eaffded42fd2cdb8cc2.svg) find a basis of $\\textrm{Null}(A).$" 13194139534305 17592186045417 true] [17592186119816 1038 #edb/ref 17592186418281 13194139534305 17592186045417 true] [17592186119817 1038 #edb/ref 17592186418279 13194139534305 17592186045417 true] [17592186119818 1038 #edb/ref 17592186418280 13194139534305 17592186045417 true] [17592186119819 1038 #edb/ref 17592186418282 13194139534305 17592186045417 true] [17592186120050 1050 960.0 13194139534305 17592186045417 true] [17592186121041 1064 "Remember that the null space $\\textrm{Null}(A)$ of a matrix $A$ consists of the solutions to the equation $A\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{0}.$ To find a basis of $\\textrm{Null}(A),$ we solve the equation $A\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{0}$ and express the solution as a linear combination of linearly independent vectors. Those vectors form a basis of $\\textrm{Null}(A).$\n\nTo illustrate, consider the matrix ![](images/70/702794c0129df672cc7c4cc65260c9bfa7e33804ea9f71bba048cf62142a0a18.svg) Computing the reduced row echelon form of $A,$ we get\n\n![](images/16/16d52c068c616e2eca149a8e81929a98c85726361d7ea2291ab314fe7567e4ce.svg)\n\nSo, the equation $A\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{0}$ is equivalent to the system\n\n![](images/bb/bb8743bd8eb072476af0e9e31c0215916beeeb82fc371348de1e6013b38810f8.svg)\n\nwhere $\\color{blue}x_1$ and $\\color{blue}x_2$ are basic variables (corresponding to the pivot columns) and $\\color{red}x_3$ is a free variable (corresponding to the non-pivot column).\n\nTherefore, any vector $\\mathbf{x}$ in $\\textrm{Null}(A)$ has the form\n\n![](images/6e/6e124e2c030f6cad72932d09211935b438f1189322ded40683a1f18618e35f3e.svg)\n\nSo, the set ![](images/24/2449d6abf866e2baa123e81c1d610940930c75c6b45ecf818d36c62191ef605f.svg) is a basis of $\\textrm{Null}(A)$ since it is a linearly independent set and the vector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ generates the null space of $A.$\n\n**Note:** It might be the case that $\\textrm{Null}(A)$ for a particular matrix $A$ equals the so-called zero subspace $\\{ \\mathbf{0} \\}$ containing only the zero-vector. The basis of $\\{ \\mathbf{0} \\}$ is said to be the *empty set*, which is a set that does not contain any vectors. The empty set is denoted by $\\emptyset$ or $\\{\\,\\}.$" 13194139534305 17592186045417 true] [17592186121041 1064 "Remember that the null space $\\textrm{Null}(A)$ of a matrix $A$ consists of the solutions to the equation $A\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{0}.$ To find a basis of $\\textrm{Null}(A),$ we solve the equation $A\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{0}$ and express the solution as a linear combination of linearly independent vectors. Those vectors form a basis of $\\textrm{Null}(A).$\n\nTo illustrate, consider the matrix $[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 3\\end{aligned}]$ Computing the reduced row echelon form of $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSo, the equation $A\\mathbf{x} = \\mathbf{0}$ is equivalent to the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}=0 \\\\ {x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0 \\\\ 0=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\color{blue}x_1$ and $\\color{blue}x_2$ are basic variables (corresponding to the pivot columns) and $\\color{red}x_3$ is a free variable (corresponding to the non-pivot column).\n\nTherefore, any vector $\\mathbf{x}$ in $\\textrm{Null}(A)$ has the form\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSo, the set $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ is a basis of $\\textrm{Null}(A)$ since it is a linearly independent set and the vector $[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ generates the null space of $A.$\n\n**Note:** It might be the case that $\\textrm{Null}(A)$ for a particular matrix $A$ equals the so-called zero subspace $\\{ \\mathbf{0} \\}$ containing only the zero-vector. The basis of $\\{ \\mathbf{0} \\}$ is said to be the *empty set*, which is a set that does not contain any vectors. The empty set is denoted by $\\emptyset$ or $\\{\\,\\}.$" 13194139534305 17592186045417 false] [17592186418255 1080 "B={[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}" 13194139534305 17592186045417 true] [17592186418255 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534305 17592186045417 true] [17592186418255 1083 #uuid "0148b837-e68e-5c80-b7ec-a73dafbfb70b" 13194139534305 17592186045417 true] [17592186418256 1080 "B={[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}" 13194139534305 17592186045417 true] [17592186418256 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534305 17592186045417 true] [17592186418256 1083 #uuid "09788694-2996-587a-98d4-8282fd5d1618" 13194139534